      "eq_exo_why": "This reaction is exothermic, so heat behaves like a product: adding heat pushes toward reactants, removing it pushes toward NH₃.",
      "eq_exothermic": "exothermic",
      "eq_expand": "Expand (V × 2)",
      "eq_group_conc": "Change an amount",
      "eq_group_temp": "Change the temperature",
      "eq_group_trap": "Common traps",
      "eq_group_vol": "Change the volume (pressure)",
      "eq_heat": "Heat (+100 K)",
      "eq_idea1_b": "Adding, removing, compressing: Q changes, K does not, and the mixture chases K. Heat or cool it and K itself moves.",
      "eq_idea1_t": "Only temperature moves K",
      "eq_idea2_b": "“Shifts right” never means “goes to completion”. Some of the added reactant is used up; the rest stays. Read the numbers, not just the arrow.",
      "eq_idea2_t": "A shift is partial",
      "eq_idea3_b": "At equilibrium N₂ and H₂ are still becoming NH₃, and NH₃ is still splitting — at exactly the same rate. Flat concentrations, busy molecules.",
      "eq_idea3_t": "Nothing has stopped",
      "eq_idea4_b": "Cooling raises the yield here, but near 300 K the reaction is far too slow to be useful. Industry accepts a smaller K at ~700 K, then adds a catalyst for speed and pressure for yield.",
      "eq_idea4_t": "Why Haber runs hot anyway",
      "eq_inert": "Add argon (same V)",
      "eq_inert_why": "Argon takes no part in the reaction. At constant volume the concentrations of N₂, H₂ and NH₃ are exactly what they were, so Q still equals K and nothing shifts.",
      "eq_k_unchanged": "and K has not moved",
      "eq_model_note": "Model: ideal gases; K(T) from the van ’t Hoff equation with ΔH° held constant. Real K values drift from this by up to a factor of ~2 at high temperature, and real plants run near 200 bar to buy yield back — this box runs at whatever pressure its moles, volume and temperature give.",
      "eq_nh3_share": "NH₃ share",
      "eq_no_shift": "No shift: Q still equals K, so the composition does not change.",
      "eq_now": "now",
      "eq_of_mixture": "of the mixture",
      "eq_q_vs_k": "Right after the stress",
      "eq_remove_nh3": "Remove half the NH₃",
      "eq_reset": "Reset: 1 mol N₂ + 3 mol H₂, 10 L, 700 K",
      "eq_shift_sentence": "The reaction runs",
      "eq_sim_title": "🧪 Stress the Haber process and watch it answer",
      "eq_status_idle": "The mixture below is at equilibrium. Pick a stress and watch which way it runs — and by how much.",
      "eq_temp_why": "Temperature is the ONE stress that changes K itself",
      "gl_box_sr": "Particle box",
      "gl_box_sr_2": "molecules drawn for",
      "gl_box_sr_3": "in a container of",
      "gl_box_sr_4": "The trail behind each molecule is its speed: root-mean-square speed is",
      "gl_box_sr_5": "metres per second at",
      "gl_calculated": "calculated",
      "gl_gas_co2": "Carbon dioxide",
      "gl_gas_he": "Helium",
      "gl_gas_n2": "Nitrogen",
      "gl_gas_o2": "Oxygen",
      "gl_graham_body": "At one temperature every gas carries the same average kinetic energy — so the light molecules must move faster to match the heavy ones. Helium is seven times lighter than nitrogen and moves about 2.6 times quicker, which is exactly √7. That is Graham's law, and it is why the helium balloon is on the floor by morning while the air-filled one is not.",
      "gl_graham_title": "🏃 Why helium escapes a balloon first",
      "gl_kelvin_body": "Heat a balloon from 25 °C to 50 °C. You doubled the number on the thermometer, so the balloon doubles in size?",
      "gl_kelvin_right": "Using K",
      "gl_kelvin_right_note": "The real answer: about 8% bigger.",
      "gl_kelvin_title": "🌡 The mistake that eats the most exam marks",
      "gl_kelvin_why": "Celsius has its zero in the wrong place. Doubling a Celsius reading does not double anything physical, because 0 °C is not \"no thermal energy\" — it is just where water freezes. Kelvin starts at true zero, so in Kelvin the ratios mean what they say. Every gas law wants Kelvin.",
      "gl_kelvin_wrong": "Using °C",
      "gl_kelvin_wrong_note": "Predicts the balloon doubles.",
      "gl_molar_volume": "Molar volume",
      "gl_molar_volume_note": "At 0 °C and 1 bar every ideal gas gives 22.71 L/mol — helium and carbon dioxide alike, because the law never asks what the molecule is.",
      "gl_moles": "Amount",
      "gl_pressure": "Pressure",
      "gl_pressure_is": "Pressure is not molecules shoving each other apart. It is how often, and how hard, they strike the walls. Squeeze the box and each molecule simply reaches a wall sooner — it never speeds up. Only temperature changes speed.",
      "gl_real_body": "The ideal law assumes molecules take up no room and ignore each other. Both assumptions hold well in a roomy, warm container and fail in a cramped, cold one. Shrink the volume or drop the temperature and watch the gap open — it opens fastest for CO₂, whose molecules attract each other most.",
      "gl_real_gap": "gap",
      "gl_real_ideal": "Ideal",
      "gl_real_title": "⚠ Where the ideal gas law stops telling the truth",
      "gl_real_vdw": "Van der Waals",
      "gl_rms": "average molecular speed",
      "gl_sim_title": "💨 Set any three. The fourth is not yours to choose.",
      "gl_solve_for": "Solve for",
      "gl_temperature": "Temperature",
      "gl_volume": "Volume",
      "gl_vs_n2": "vs N₂",
      "gl_which_gas": "Which gas"
