      "winter_animals": "WINTER + ANIMALS",
      "winter_content": "Maine winters test every skill you have. Four rules: (1) Look far ahead. Bad things happen slowly if you see them coming. Tight focus = surprise = crashes. (2) Gentle inputs. Smooth acceleration, smooth braking, smooth steering. Jerky = slip. (3) Increase following distance to at least 6 seconds on snow and 8 on ice (this is Road Ready's guidance; the Maine manual says at least 4 seconds, and more on slick roads). The regular 4 seconds is not enough when friction drops to 0.2. (4) When in doubt, slow down more. Maine-specific: moose warnings are real — the risk runs from dusk to dawn and peaks in May and June, scan treelines. Black ice on bridges FIRST. Take reasonable measures so snow and ice cannot fall from your car; safest practice is clearing the roof, windows, lights, and hood before driving. Keep your gas tank above half (prevents fuel line freeze, and a half tank means real emergency reserves if you get stuck).",
      "winter_driving": "Winter Driving",
