      "hi4_arc_context": "Choose an arc process to compare voltage and current.",
      "hi4_axes": "Across: voltage, 10–40 V. Upward: current, 50–350 A. The curve shows only pairs within those fixed bounds.",
      "hi4_bounds": "The calculator spans 10–40 V and 50–350 A. Holding this product leaves approximately {value1}–{value2} V available in that numeric domain.",
      "hi4_candidate": "2 · Local candidate",
      "hi4_caption": "The reference ring stays fixed while the candidate dot moves. Raising voltage along this curve lowers current, keeping their product unchanged.",
      "hi4_capture": "Capture calculator settings",
      "hi4_fixed": "Captured: {value1}; speed {value2} in/min; mild-steel plate {value3} in. These stay fixed in this exchange.",
      "hi4_gross": "Gross heat input",
      "hi4_identity": "Candidate: {value1} V × {value2} A ≈ {value3} V·A. With speed and process fixed, both gross and transferred kJ/in stay the same in this calculation.",
      "hi4_intro": "For arc-process inputs, capture the calculator settings to hold a local reference. Then exchange voltage for current while holding their product, speed, process and plate fixed. Exploring here leaves the calculator, energy run and notebook unchanged.",
      "hi4_lower": "Lower bound",
      "hi4_model_cap": "Model cap height",
      "hi4_model_penetration": "Model penetration",
      "hi4_model_width": "Model width",
      "hi4_net": "Transferred heat input",
      "hi4_note": "These paired sections use the existing classroom bead model. Both sections use the same plate and drawing scale. The full model penetration is listed; the section stops at the bottom of the plate. Equal transferred heat input gives equal penetration in this simplified model, while width and cap height can differ. This comparison does not establish equivalent welds or procedures, recommend settings, or predict real weld performance. Readouts are rounded; calculations and geometry retain unrounded values.",
      "hi4_plot_aria": "Fixed voltage-current axes. The white ring marks the captured pair. The filled dot marks approximately {value1} volts and {value2} amperes at the same voltage-current product.",
      "hi4_presets": "Feasible voltage references",
