      "run_tier_biw_desc": "Ship hull: tight band, fastest travel, the biggest gap and heat build-up.",
      "run_tier_pro": "Pro",
      "run_tier_pro_desc": "Production line: tighter band, bigger drift, a tack weld to cross.",
      "run_tip_clean": "{value1}% of the bead in spec. Try the next tier: a tighter band and bigger disturbances.",
      "run_tip_ctwd_long": "While your stickout drifted long at {value1} in., the arc lost up to about {value2} A and only {value3}% stayed in spec. Nudge the amps up or slow a little; in the booth you would also bring the torch back down.",
      "run_tip_ctwd_short": "When your stickout got short at {value1} in., the arc gained up to about {value2} A and only {value3}% stayed in spec. Ease the amps down or speed up a little.",
      "run_tip_gap": "Over the fit-up gap at {value1} in., only {value2}% of the bead stayed in spec. With less metal under the arc it digs deeper: speed up or lower the amps across a gap.",
      "run_tip_heat": "The plate climbed to {value1} °F and the last quarter of the bead ran deep. A long joint heats up: ease the travel speed up as you go.",
      "run_tip_jerky": "Your bead width wandered (uniformity {value1}%). Make small, early corrections instead of big, late ones.",
      "run_tip_step_good": "You crossed the thickness step cleanly (back in spec within {value1} in.). That is reading the joint ahead of the puddle.",
      "run_tip_step_late": "When the plate thinned at {value1} in., it took {value2} in. of travel to get back in spec. Watch the step line ahead and start speeding up as the puddle reaches it.",
      "run_tip_step_never": "After the plate thinned at {value1} in., the bead never came back into spec. Thin plate needs about half the heat per inch: speed up or turn the amps down as the puddle reaches the step line.",
      "run_tip_tack": "Crossing the tack weld at {value1} in., only {value2}% stayed in spec. The tack soaks up heat: slow down briefly to fuse into it.",
      "run_tips_h": "Coaching",
      "run_title": "Live Bead Run",
      "run_tl_h": "Timeline along the joint",
      "run_tl_key_ev": "Numbered circles: events",
      "run_tl_key_in": "Short solid bar: in spec",
      "run_tl_key_out": "Tall striped bar: out of spec",
      "run_tn_ext": "Run the same tier three times and record the in-spec score, the travel speed you used on each plate thickness, and the plate temperature at the end. Plot travel speed against plate thickness and against plate temperature. Then open the Bead Lab, set the same volts and amps, and check whether your thin-plate travel speed lands its penetration near 0.6-0.75 of 1/8 in. Representative classroom numbers; a real procedure comes from the WPS for the joint.",
      "run_tn_m1": "\"Set the machine right and the weld takes care of itself.\" The joint changes under the torch (thickness, fit-up, heat build-up), so the welder's hands are part of the control loop.",
      "run_tn_m2": "\"Slower is always safer.\" Slowing down adds heat per inch: on thin plate or over a gap it causes burn-through.",
      "run_tn_m3": "\"Stickout does not matter.\" On a constant-voltage MIG machine a longer stickout lowers the current, so penetration sags even with the same settings.",
      "run_tn_m4": "\"A high score means a fast run.\" The score is the share of the bead in spec, not the time taken.",
      "run_tn_q1": "The plate thins from 1/4 in. to 1/8 in. halfway along. Using heat input = V × A × 60 ÷ travel speed, explain why speeding up is the fix, and estimate how much faster you must go to halve the heat per inch.",
      "run_tn_q2": "Two students both score 70%. One lost points at the thickness step, the other through heat build-up at the end. What different habits would you coach each of them on?",
      "run_tn_q3": "Why does a real welding procedure (WPS) give a RANGE for travel speed and amperage rather than one number? Connect your answer to the disturbances in this run.",
      "run_tn_q4": "A shipyard tier has the tightest band and the biggest disturbances. Why would a hull weld demand more consistency than a shop bracket?",
      "run_to_beadlab": "Try volts, amps and travel speed in the Bead Lab →",
