      "sym11_at_midpoint": "P is at the midpoint: the root-to-P segment is perpendicular to the face and reaches the minimum distance.",
      "sym11_intro": "Use an ideal equal-leg fillet with a straight face. Move point P along that face and compare the root-to-P segment with the perpendicular segment to the midpoint. The theoretical throat is the shortest root-to-straight-face distance.",
      "sym11_leg_group": "Ideal fillet leg size",
      "sym11_legend": "The outlined point at the inside corner is the joint root. Toe 1 ends the horizontal leg; toe 2 ends the vertical leg. The straight face joins the toes. The filled point P and solid segment move together. The dashed segment is the theoretical throat; its square corner marks the perpendicular. At the midpoint, the segments coincide.",
      "sym11_midpoint": "Move P to the midpoint",
      "sym11_off_midpoint": "P is away from the midpoint: this root-to-P segment is longer than the perpendicular minimum.",
      "sym11_plot_aria": "Ideal equal-leg fillet, each leg {value1} inches. Point P is {value2}% along the straight face. Root-to-P distance is about {value3} inches; theoretical throat is about {value4} inches. The minimum segment meets the straight face at a right angle.",
      "sym11_probe_aria": "{value1}% of the straight face from toe 1 toward toe 2",
      "sym11_probe_label": "Point P along the straight face, from toe 1 toward toe 2",
      "sym11_ratio_label": "Theoretical throat ÷ leg = 1 ÷ √2 ≈ 0.707107.",
      "sym11_read_leg": "Each equal leg",
      "sym11_read_probe": "Root-to-P distance",
      "sym11_read_throat": "Theoretical throat",
      "sym11_scope": "This planar reference uses equal legs and a straight face, without root penetration or convex or concave weld faces. Every leg size and probe position uses one fixed drawing scale. Readings are rounded to four decimals; geometry uses unrounded values. It illustrates theoretical throat geometry, not weld strength or acceptance.",
      "sym11_title": "Explore fillet leg and throat geometry",
