      "initializing": "🛰️ Docking with the station…",
      "inside_cabin": "Inside a crew sleep cabin (Harmony)",
      "inside_cupola": "Inside the Cupola",
      "interior_intro": "Move through five real station spaces. Inspect what is around you, make a crew decision, and learn the science from the result.",
      "interior_kicker": "Your crew shift",
      "interior_route": "Interior station route",
      "interior_title": "Float inside. Work like an astronaut.",
      "later": "Later",
      "legacy": "🌍 Why it matters",
      "legacy_body": "The station is the most expensive object ever built (well over $100 billion) and arguably the most ambitious peacetime engineering partnership in history: 15 nations keeping hardware and crews alive together for a quarter century, through every political storm on the ground below. Its deepest lesson is not any single experiment — it is proof that humans can build and operate a permanent home off Earth. Everything learned aboard, from water recycling to bone loss, is a down payment on the Moon and Mars.",
      "map_intro": "A schematic (not to scale) 3-D map of the real station, showing an illustrative Dragon at Harmony, Progress at Zvezda and Cygnus berthed at Unity. This is a historical teaching arrangement, not a live visitor manifest. Drag or use the arrow keys to orbit the camera. Choose Pan to slide the view with one finger or the mouse, or hold Shift while dragging or using arrow keys. Pinch or use plus and minus to zoom, and press F to center the selected module. Focus the canvas to use the scroll wheel for zoom. Home returns to the overview. Camera movement preserves the station’s attitude and lighting. The module buttons provide an equivalent non-canvas inspection path.",
      "mission_dock": "Mission 1 — Dock the cargo capsule",
      "mission_eva": "Mission 2 — Spacewalk: replace the failed pump",
      "mission_report": "Mission report: ",
      "missions_intro": "Two hands-on missions. Both are simplified but honest: the docking sim runs the real relative-motion equations (with orbit effects sped up so you can feel them), and the spacewalk enforces the real two-tether safety rule.",
      "note_height": "Spines stretch without gravity — astronauts return up to ~3 cm taller (temporarily, and often with backaches).",
      "note_laundry": "There is no laundry. Clothes are worn for days, then packed into cargo ships that burn up on reentry as shooting stars.",
      "note_sunrise": "16 sunrises and sunsets every day — crews use clocks and window shades, not the sky, to know when to sleep.",
      "note_taste": "Food tastes bland aboard: fluid shifts stuff up sinuses like a permanent head cold, so hot sauce and shrimp cocktail are prized.",
      "numbers": "By the numbers: ",
      "orbit_intro": "Being in orbit is not about being high — it is about being FAST. The station falls around Earth continuously; the crew floats because station and astronaut fall together. Slide the altitude and watch real physics (v = √(GM/r)) respond.",
      "orbit_lab": "🧮 Orbit designer",
      "orbit_note": "Notice the counter-intuitive part: LOWER orbits are FASTER. To catch up with something ahead of you in orbit, you briefly slow down and drop lower. Orbital mechanics breaks driving intuition — which is why dockings are computed, not eyeballed.",
      "orbit_status": "Current station reference data",
      "quiz_again": "🔁 Run it again",
      "quiz_done": "🏁 Debrief",
      "quiz_finish": "Finish →",
      "quiz_great": "Flight-controller material. Quest objective complete!",
      "quiz_next": "Next →",
      "quiz_retry": "Every controller trains on repetitions — revisit the tabs and fly it again.",
      "quiz_toast": "Station quiz aced: ",
      "scene_aurora": "🟢 Aurora pass",
      "scene_day": "☀️ Daylit Pacific",
      "scene_night": "🌃 Night — city lights",
      "sections": "Space Station sections",
      "shutter_note": "Every window has an external shutter, closed whenever the Cupola is unattended — a micrometeoroid pit in this glass would be very bad news.",
      "shutters_closed": "Shutters closed",
      "spot_bag": "Sleeping bag",
      "spot_gear": "Personal kit",
      "spot_laptop": "Laptop",
      "spot_note": "💡 You can SEE the station: it is the third-brightest object in the sky after the Sun and Moon. NASA’s \"Spot the Station\" service lists pass times for your town — it looks like a brilliant star crossing the sky in a few minutes, moving too smoothly to be a plane.",
      "spot_photos": "Family photos",
      "spot_vent": "Air vent",
      "subtitle": "The International Space Station: 420 tonnes of engineering falling around the planet at 7.66 km/s, continuously inhabited for over 25 years.",
      "sys_intro": "The station is a closed-loop machine that must make its own air, recycle its own water, shed its own heat, and hold its own orientation — forever, with no hardware store. Each system below is an engineering-design case study (NGSS MS-ETS1).",
