      "dx_lionfish_habitat": "Coral reefs and rocky reef shelter, with crevices available between feeding trips.",
      "dx_lionfish_observe": "Identify the striped body and separate long back spines while giving its spread fins plenty of space.",
      "dx_long_spined_urchin_behavior": "Its long movable spines defend a small central body while it travels over the bottom.",
      "dx_long_spined_urchin_diet": "Grazes algae from dead coral and rubble, helping keep exposed reef surfaces cropped.",
      "dx_long_spined_urchin_habitat": "Shallow coral and rocky reefs, with crevices that offer daytime shelter.",
      "dx_long_spined_urchin_observe": "Observe the five pale spots and orange-ringed raised cone from a distance clear of the spines.",
      "dx_magnificent_anemone_behavior": "Its soft body can contract into a rounded ball while clownfish use the tentacles as shelter.",
      "dx_magnificent_anemone_diet": "Receives energy from symbiotic algae and captures small animals with stinging tentacles.",
      "dx_magnificent_anemone_habitat": "Clear tropical reef water, usually anchored to exposed rock where currents reach its tentacles.",
      "dx_magnificent_anemone_observe": "Look at the visible colored column and rounded tentacle tips, then watch the clownfish partnership.",
      "dx_mandarin_dragonet_behavior": "Usually stays close to the bottom, sometimes in pairs or loose local groups. At dusk, courting pairs may rise together into the water to spawn.",
      "dx_mandarin_dragonet_diet": "Tiny bottom-dwelling invertebrates, including copepods and other small crustaceans picked from sediment and reef crevices.",
      "dx_mandarin_dragonet_habitat": "Sheltered reefs and lagoons around the western Pacific, from southern Japan through Southeast Asia to northern Australia and nearby island groups. Often close to coral rubble and other shelter.",
      "dx_mandarin_dragonet_observe": "Trace the winding colour bands, then inspect the broad head and fan-shaped tail. The long first dorsal spine belongs to the adult male shown here; females have a shorter first dorsal fin.",
      "dx_meet": "Meet {name}.",
      "dx_moorish_idol_behavior": "Often travels as a pair, although larger gatherings also occur.",
      "dx_moorish_idol_diet": "Picks mainly at encrusting sponges, with coral polyps, tunicates and other small attached animals.",
      "dx_moorish_idol_habitat": "Coastal and offshore coral and rocky reefs, including reef lagoons.",
      "dx_moorish_idol_observe": "Trace the long white dorsal streamer, tube-shaped snout and yellow saddle across the nose.",
      "dx_more_life": "Explore more reef life",
      "dx_mushroom_coral_behavior": "A single large polyp forms the disc; adults are not permanently cemented to the reef.",
      "dx_mushroom_coral_diet": "Algal partners supply energy, and the animal polyp also captures tiny drifting food.",
      "dx_mushroom_coral_habitat": "Reef slopes and lagoons, on sand or rubble where the adult lies loose on the bottom.",
