      "fig_ac_title": "Aragonite saturation",
      "fig_bl_aria": "Diagram. A healthy coral kept in water that is too warm for too long loses its algae and turns white: it is bleached, alive but starving. If the water cools in time, the algae return. If the heat goes on too long, the coral dies. Below, the 2016 Great Barrier Reef heatwave: reefs with under about 3 Degree Heating Weeks lost almost no coral, and reefs with about 8 lost about two thirds of their coral within eight months.",
      "fig_bl_at8": "About 8 DHW",
